            <ArticleTitle>Role of lipoprotein-associated phospholipase A2 in atherosclerosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>230-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Lipoprotein-associated phospholipase A(2)(Lp-PLA(2)) is a biomarker that can be used to assess the risk for cardiovascular disease and events. In addition to being a useful marker of a risk factor, several studies suggest that Lp-PLA(2) has a pathophysiologic role in the atherosclerotic disease process. In this article, we review this aspect and its therapeutic implications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
